  "response": "14년 동안 쌓아온 자기상이 흔들리는 걸 느끼시는 거죠. 그건 꽤 불편한 일이에요.\n\n그런데 \"방어\"라는 단어를 쓰셨을 때, 혹시 그 원칙이 지켜지지 않으면 어떤 일이 생길 것 같은 느낌이 드는 순간이 있으셨나요?",
  "reasoning": "The user is a counselor responding to a client in a counseling session. Let me analyze what the client has shared:\n\n1. The client publicly demanded a \"recurrence prevention document\" from a colleague who had a minor omission in paperwork, during a meeting.\n2. The colleague now avoids eye contact with the client.\n3. The client has been known as someone who does things \"by the principles\" for 14 years at work.\n4. The client is now questioning whether this \"principle\" is actually a defense mechanism.\n5. The client wants to understand why they get so defensive/aggressive.\n\nThis is a rich opening. The client has already done significant self-reflection - they're not just reporting an event but questioning the pattern itself. They've identified a possible defense mechanism and are seeking understanding.\n\nWhat would be a good counselor response here?\n\n- I should acknowledge the self-awareness they've shown (recognizing the \"principle\" might be a defense)\n- I shouldn't rush to solutions or validate them too quickly\n- I shouldn't just reflect back what they said\n- I could gently explore the connection between the \"principle\" identity and the defensive function\n- I should be careful not to assume what they're defending against\n\nThe client has already made a significant insight - that their \"principle\" might be a defense. A good response would honor that insight while perhaps gently exploring it further. I could ask about what they feel is at stake when principles aren't followed, or what it means to them personally. But I should be careful not to be too directive.\n\nLet me think about what's most helpful here. The client came wanting to understand \"why\" they get so defensive. They've already offered a hypothesis (defense mechanism). A good counselor might:\n1. Acknowledge the courage of that self-observation\n2. Gently explore what the \"principle\" protects - what would it mean if they weren't the \"principled person\"?\n\nI'll keep it concise, warm, and exploratory without being pushy. One question, focused on what feels most alive in what they just shared.\n",
  "started_at": "2026-11-07T11:57:32.573258+00:00",
